    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Legal scholars often critique the dissent for relying too heavily on a broad, abstract concept of \"liberty\" without grounding it in existing constitutional text or precedent. They argue the dissent fails to demonstrate *where* in the Constitution a fundamental, right to abortion is explicitly or implicitly guaranteed, making the claim speculative rather than legally sound. Furthermore, many scholars point out that the dissent insufficiently addressed the structural reality of the past jurisprudence, which had already established a legal framework for balancing state interests and bodily autonomy. This over-reliance on broad rights claims without clear constitutional textual support is the primary academic critique.",
      "source": "target",
